() Tj T*
(The great and fundamental mistake made in the initiation of the) Tj T*
(English Insurance Act was that in effect it ignored the entire) Tj T*
(history of the relation of preventive and curative medicine to) Tj T*
(the State. This history cannot be detailed now: but, briefly, for) Tj T*
(long years the destitute had been entitled to domiciliary and) Tj T*
(institutional treatment at the public expense. This medical aid was) Tj T*
(given by Poor Law Authorities, and their method of doing this work) Tj T*
(had rendered the benefaction commonly unacceptable. Then Public) Tj T*
(Health Authorities on a steadily increasing scale found it necessary) Tj T*
(to treat disease in order the more effectively to prevent it. And so) Tj T*
(fevers and smallpox, and chronic infective diseases like tuberculosis) Tj T*
(and syphilis came under treatment, practically for all comers, at) Tj T*
(the public expense. As already mentioned the fundamental importance) Tj T*
(of maternity and childhood has also been realised, and the State is) Tj T*
(now taking an increasing share in ensuring health at these periods) Tj T*
(of life. And while Public Health Authorities were increasing their) Tj T*
(activities, Education Authorities began to subject school children) Tj T*
(to medical inspection, and to treat them for the detected defects,) Tj T*
(the treatment of which they could not otherwise secure. And so, not) Tj T*
(to make this sketch too complex, three great central government) Tj T*
(departments or sub-departments and three sets of local authorities) Tj T*
(were engaged in medically treating the people at the public expense.) Tj T*
