(On examining the local city directory, one cannot but be impressed) Tj T*
(by the multiplicity of voluntary organizations having for their) Tj T*
(object the immediate relief of destitution or the social or economic) Tj T*
(?uplift? of sections of the population. The multiplicity of these) Tj T*
(agencies becomes more striking when one remembers that probably) Tj T*
(every one of the hundreds of churches and chapels in the city has) Tj T*
(its periodical sacramental and other collections for the poor, and) Tj T*
(may have also a system of parochial district visiting, with such) Tj T*
(auxiliary assistance as is provided through mothers? meetings, etc.) Tj T*
(Nor does this exhaust the possibilities of social help available) Tj T*
(for the poorer members of society in cities in which there is a) Tj T*
(satisfactory distribution of rich and poor, that is, in which the) Tj T*
(segregation of different social strata in separate areas has happily) Tj T*
(not befallen. There is the further help provided by individual) Tj T*
(charity, the amount of which in the aggregate probably exceeds) Tj T*
(beneficence through churches and social agencies.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(If these different agencies could pool their resources, while) Tj T*
(retaining the enthusiasm and driving power of separate organization,) Tj T*
(what an economy of effort and what increase of efficiency would) Tj T*
(result, especially if these agencies were also satisfactorily related) Tj T*
(to the official organizations of local and central governing bodies) Tj T*
(having the same object!) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(But I am not concerned this evening to discuss the machinery of) Tj T*
(social help or the attempts already made in different centres for) Tj T*
(securing their satisfactory coöperation. Nor am I disposed to discuss) Tj T*
(the economic problems underlying the need for social assistance of) Tj T*
(the poor. Ideally we must agree with St. Augustine?s statement:) Tj T*
(?Thou givest bread to the hungry; but better were it, that none) Tj T*
(hungered, and that thou had?st none to give him.? My present object,) Tj T*
(however, is to set out some elementary?and when stated fairly) Tj T*
(obvious?considerations bearing on social evils and their remedies) Tj T*
(under present conditions of society, the recollection of which) Tj T*
(if followed by practical action, would secure greatly increased) Tj T*
(efficiency in social work.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(For the following reasons I do not hesitate to bring this subject) Tj T*
(before a gathering of graduate medical students:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(First.?Every physician as soon as he engages in medical practice) Tj T*
(almost immediately comes into touch with organized and unorganized) Tj T*
