(                            CHAPTER VIII) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(                     SOME ASPECTS OF POVERTY[16]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(I use the word Poverty, for the purpose of this discussion, as) Tj T*
(meaning Destitution, in the sense of lack of means to provide some) Tj T*
(specific requirement, indispensable for the health of the family, or) Tj T*
(the individual.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Such poverty is evidently undesirable and mischievous, from the point) Tj T*
(of view of both rich and poor; and I think we shall agree that, given) Tj T*
(the adoption of the requisite measures, its continuance in most) Tj T*
(instances is unnecessary. Hence the real subject for discussion is,) Tj T*
(how poverty may be diminished and prevented.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(I do not propose to touch on the important subjects of unemployment,) Tj T*
(of under-employment, or of the relation between the size of family) Tj T*
(and poverty, though the last named of these opens up an interesting) Tj T*
(subject of discussion. \(On this see page 164.\) I shall confine my) Tj T*
(remarks to the very obvious relation between poverty and sickness,) Tj T*
(and to the neglect to act on our present knowledge, which if acted) Tj T*
(on would in a short time lead to a great reduction of poverty in our) Tj T*
(midst.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There is much truth in each of the statements that poverty is) Tj T*
(responsible for much disease, that disease is responsible for the) Tj T*
(greater part of the total poverty in our midst, and that poverty) Tj T*
(begets poverty.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Poverty and disease are allied by the closest bonds, and nothing can) Tj T*
(be simpler or more certain than the statement that the removal of) Tj T*
(poverty would effect an enormous reduction of disease. The removal of) Tj T*
(poverty must, therefore, be in itself an object always fascinating) Tj T*
(to those whose study is the public health. The diseases which would) Tj T*
(be reduced by this means, include not merely those which physicians) Tj T*
(treat, but many moral diseases which persist because they are only) Tj T*
(to be avoided by the poor through the exercise of discipline and) Tj T*
(self-restraint far beyond what is practised by the average person) Tj T*
(in classes not subject to poverty. The happiness of a community) Tj T*
(being in itself a desirable object, a national asset, it is also not) Tj T*
(irrelevant to consider that the removal of poverty involves enlarged) Tj T*
(opportunities for enjoyment which, rightly directed, would be only) Tj T*
(of less value than the removal of disease. It is not surprising,) Tj T*
(therefore, that the first impulse of a student of the public) Tj T*
(well-being, in which the public health is the most important factor,) Tj T*
(is to attack disease by demanding the reduction of poverty, with its) Tj T*
