(tuberculosis?which is responsible for 71 per cent. of the total) Tj T*
(mortality from tuberculosis, and which is practically always due to) Tj T*
(infection from a human source,?declined in males between 1871-75) Tj T*
(and 1876-80 by 7.2 per cent.; in the next quinquennium by 9.8 per) Tj T*
(cent.; between 1881-85 and 1886-90 by 8.3 per cent.; in the next) Tj T*
(quinquennium by 9.5 per cent.; between 1896-1900 and 1901-05 by 7 per) Tj T*
(cent.; and between 1901-06 and 1906-10 by 9.7 per cent. Evidently a) Tj T*
(large share of the reduction of the death-rate from phthisis occurred) Tj T*
(before it was generally regarded as an infectious disease, and before) Tj T*
(sanatoria were in existence for its treatment. It should be added) Tj T*
(that since the possibilities of infection have been realised and) Tj T*
(the need for treatment of the disease has been appreciated, there) Tj T*
