(                   _Scope of Tuberculosis Schemes_) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Prior to the general enforcement of notification of tuberculosis) Tj T*
(in England excellent local work had been done in a relatively) Tj T*
(small number of areas in direct efforts to control the spread of) Tj T*
(tuberculosis, in addition to the previous general measures, such as) Tj T*
(improved sanitation, better housing, more satisfactory nutrition, and) Tj T*
(especially the hospital treatment of a large proportion of advanced) Tj T*
(and acute cases of tuberculosis. The Report of the last Royal) Tj T*
(Commission on Tuberculosis appeared in 1911; and although precautions) Tj T*
(against human infection by tuberculous cows? milk are still very) Tj T*
(incomplete, the pasteurisation or boiling of milk is more generally) Tj T*
(practised than in the past.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Local Authorities prior to 1911 had power to build sanatoria or) Tj T*
(otherwise provide institutional accommodations for the treatment) Tj T*
(of tuberculous patients; relatively little had been done in most) Tj T*
(areas. In 1911 the Finance Act provided a sum of £1,116,000 for the) Tj T*
(erection of sanatoria in England and Wales, and this, with money) Tj T*
(provided by local rates, has led to rapid increase in accommodation) Tj T*
(for the residential institutional treatment of tuberculosis. In) Tj T*
(England in 1911 local authorities, other than poor-law authorities,) Tj T*
(had about 1300 beds for the institutional treatment of tuberculosis,) Tj T*
(while there were 4,200 beds in private sanatoria and voluntary) Tj T*
(institutions. In 1917 the total available beds numbered 12,441, of) Tj T*
(which about one-half had been provided by local authorities.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In 1911 the National Insurance Act was passed and came into operation) Tj T*
(in July, 1912. This provided a special ?Sanatorium Benefit.?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The Departmental Committee appointed to make recommendations as to) Tj T*
(detailed direct measures against tuberculosis, reported in April,) Tj T*
(1912, that any scheme which is to form the basis of an attempt to) Tj T*
(deal with the problem of tuberculosis should be available for the) Tj T*
(whole community, and that its organization should be undertaken by) Tj T*
(the large local authorities \(the councils of counties and county) Tj T*
(boroughs\). These recommendations were at once adopted by the) Tj T*
(Government, which undertook to provide out of the national exchequer) Tj T*
(one-half of the net cost of approved local schemes for the general) Tj T*
(treatment of tuberculosis. Local authorities were invited at once) Tj T*
(to prepare schemes for institutional treatment, residential and) Tj T*
(non-residential, domiciliary treatment remaining in the hands of) Tj T*
(private practitioners, of poor-law doctors, and of doctors engaged in) Tj T*
(the contract work under the National Insurance Act \(?panel doctors?\).) Tj T*
(The last named are in medical charge of the large mass of the) Tj T*
(wage-earners of the community, comprising roughly one-third of the) Tj T*
(total population, in so far as their treatment at home is within the) Tj T*
