(power of a practitioner of average competence. The schemes proposed) Tj T*
(for each area comprised,) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. The appointment of a tuberculosis officer, usually a whole-time) Tj T*
(official, who was required to have had special experience in the) Tj T*
(diagnosis and treatment of tuberculosis, and who as a rule was an) Tj T*
(officer in the public-health department under the administrative) Tj T*
(supervision of the medical officer of health, but independent in his) Tj T*
(clinical work;) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. The establishment of tuberculosis dispensaries, at which patients) Tj T*
(were treated, consultations as to doubtful cases held, and contacts) Tj T*
(examined;) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(3. The provision of beds in residential institutions for curable and) Tj T*
(for acute and advanced cases;) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(4. The organization of arrangements for ?following up? and) Tj T*
(?after-care.?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(During 1912 and 1913 advance was made in these directions. In 1911) Tj T*
(there were 25-30 tuberculosis dispensaries: in 1917 their number) Tj T*
(had increased to 371. In 1914 the onset of the Great War prevented) Tj T*
(further development of tuberculosis work and seriously crippled and) Tj T*
(reduced the efficiency of work already initiated; and this increased) Tj T*
(as the military demand for medical officers and institutions became) Tj T*
(greater. It may be stated generally that in only a relatively) Tj T*
(small number of areas have fairly complete arrangements for the) Tj T*
(institutional treatment of tuberculosis come into operation; and) Tj T*
(that even in these areas the arrangements have been at work for only) Tj T*
(a limited period. It is evident, therefore, as already pointed out,) Tj T*
(that no argument as to the utility of these arrangements can be based) Tj T*
(on the facts that the death-rate from tuberculosis has not declined) Tj T*
(with increased rapidity in recent years, and that women during the) Tj T*
(war, especially at the working years of life have experienced an) Tj T*
(increased death-rate from this disease.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(                     _Tuberculosis Dispensaries_) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The tuberculosis officer is the essential element in the dispensary;) Tj T*
(and in rural districts he may be said to carry the dispensary under) Tj T*
(his hat. The dispensary if properly organized should serve as the) Tj T*
(centre of official anti-tuberculosis measures. The medical officer of) Tj T*
(health receives the notifications of recognised cases whether they) Tj T*
(are attending the dispensary or not; and it simplifies administration) Tj T*
(if the home supervision of all tuberculous patients notified to) Tj T*
(the medical officer of health, and not only of dispensary patients,) Tj T*
(is placed under the supervision of the tuberculosis officer. At) Tj T*
(the dispensary itself the tuberculosis officer examines patients,) Tj T*
(makes records of their condition, and of all facts bearing on their) Tj T*
(welfare, and recommends the special form of continued treatment) Tj T*
