(Soon after the passing of the National Insurance Act in 1911) Tj T*
(representations were made that tuberculosis affected non-insured as) Tj T*
(well as insured; that treatment of insured could have only partial) Tj T*
(success so long as non-insured members of the same household were) Tj T*
(neglected; and that this was work for public health authorities which) Tj T*
(they were already partially undertaking. It was evident that the) Tj T*
(inextricably interlaced measures for the prevention and the treatment) Tj T*
(of tuberculosis must accrue to the whole population; and the mistake) Tj T*
(of the National Insurance Act was remedied to the extent that) Tj T*
(Public Health Authorities were informed that the National Treasury) Tj T*
(was prepared to pay one-half of the approved expenditure incurred) Tj T*
(by these authorities in establishing schemes for the treatment of) Tj T*
(tuberculosis available for the entire population. Such schemes) Tj T*
(were proceeded with, as already indicated; but there remained the) Tj T*
(fact that insured persons who had paid their weekly quota and were) Tj T*
(therefore entitled to ?Sanatorium Benefit? usually interpreted this) Tj T*
(as a right to three months? treatment in a Sanatorium. The choice of) Tj T*
(persons to receive treatment in a Sanatorium lay with Local Insurance) Tj T*
(Committees appointed under the National Insurance Act, who generally) Tj T*
(acted on the advice of the tuberculosis officer; but influences) Tj T*
(other than medical led to the unsatisfactory use of institutional) Tj T*
(treatment. A large number of patients were sent to and retained) Tj T*
(in sanatoria for prolonged periods, who might have been adequately) Tj T*
(treated at home, or who should have been in hospitals. Satisfactory) Tj T*
(results for sanatorium treatment were not secured under these) Tj T*
(conditions; and there will probably be no material improvement until) Tj T*
