(          _General Observations on Treatment in Sanatoria_) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In 1911 the extent and limitations of the utility of sanatorium) Tj T*
(treatment of tuberculosis were already fairly well recognized by) Tj T*
(physicians; and it is unfortunate that in connection with the) Tj T*
(passage of the National Insurance Act this treatment acquired a) Tj T*
(somewhat political aspect, and became the subject of much popular) Tj T*
(misapprehension and exaggeration. Disappointment necessarily followed) Tj T*
(on the sending of patients to sanatoria for treatment with a view to) Tj T*
(cure at a stage of disease when anything beyond ephemeral improvement) Tj T*
(was impossible. The patients who, under present conditions, are) Tj T*
(admitted to sanatoria come roughly into two groups:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(First. Patients with limited disease and little or no systemic) Tj T*
(disturbance. Comparatively few patients who now enter sanatoria come) Tj T*
(within this group.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Second. Patients with more extensive or acute disease. In a large) Tj T*
(proportion of cases within the first group the immediate result) Tj T*
(of sanatorium treatment extending over three to six months is the) Tj T*
(complete restoration of general health and working capacity with) Tj T*
(arrest of disease. In a large further proportion of cases in the same) Tj T*
(group there is recovery of working capacity and apparent restoration) Tj T*
(of general health without complete arrest of disease.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(For patients coming within the second group a similar period of) Tj T*
(treatment in a sanatorium results:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(\(_a_\) In restoration of general health and working capacity with) Tj T*
(arrest of disease in only a small proportion of cases;) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(\(_b_\) In recovery of working capacity and apparent restoration of) Tj T*
(general health without arrest of disease in a fair proportion of) Tj T*
(cases; and) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(\(_c_\) In the remainder, disease progresses steadily with or without) Tj T*
(temporary improvement in general health.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The subsequent history of sanatorium patients varies greatly. Some) Tj T*
(of them maintain their health indefinitely on return to their) Tj T*
(ordinary life. Others who have been discharged with arrested disease) Tj T*
(ultimately relapse, even if they live under excellent environmental) Tj T*
(conditions; and such relapses are excessive among those who return to) Tj T*
(unsatisfactory conditions of life and work.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
