(_Summary_.?The preceding review of the problem of tuberculosis may be) Tj T*
(summarised in a few final statements.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. Our knowledge of tuberculosis, if fully applied by combined attack) Tj T*
(on the disease by all known methods, is adequate to secure a great) Tj T*
(reduction in its prevalence, if not its absolute abolition.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This is true, although certain problems respecting tuberculosis) Tj T*
(still need elucidation, e.g., as to improved methods of treating the) Tj T*
(diseases, and of increasing individual immunity during exposure to) Tj T*
(protracted infection.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. Domestic protection is at once practicable against infected cows?) Tj T*
(milk; and control of this source of infection at its source is also) Tj T*
(practicable.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(3. Of the circumstances favouring the development of pulmonary) Tj T*
(tuberculosis industrial dust and domestic overcrowding are the most) Tj T*
(potent. More detailed and systematic supervision of factories and) Tj T*
(workshops is needed, followed by general adoption of remedies, which) Tj T*
(would increase industrial efficiency as well as reduce tuberculosis.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(4. Tuberculosis is especially a ?bedroom infection.? But improvement) Tj T*
(in housing is a dual problem, and it is a blunder to assume that) Tj T*
(improved housing, so long as the healthy and tuberculous sick) Tj T*
(continue to be housed together, will produce a rapid decline in the) Tj T*
(prevalence of tuberculosis. Hospital provision for the sick is as) Tj T*
(necessary as improved general housing.) Tj T*
