(                              CHAPTER X) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(                  CHILD WELFARE WORK IN ENGLAND[18]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The subject of child welfare, in its chief developments, cannot be) Tj T*
(separated from that of Public Health, of which it forms a constituent) Tj T*
(part, though I do not ignore the fact that child welfare is largely) Tj T*
(dependent also on the extent to which child labor is exploited, and) Tj T*
(to which expectant and nursing mothers,?as also other mothers whose) Tj T*
(extra-domestic employment or whose employment for gain is within the) Tj T*
(home itself,?involves neglect of young children.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Improvement in child welfare has occurred as the sanitary and social) Tj T*
(progress of the country has advanced. Whereas in the decade 1871-80,) Tj T*
(when money began to be spent more freely on elementary sanitary) Tj T*
(reform, the expectation of life or mean after-lifetime at birth of) Tj T*
(males was 41.4 years and of females was 44.6 years; in the years) Tj T*
(1910-12 these had increased to 51.5 and 55.4 years respectively. The) Tj T*
(greater part of the saving of life which this addition of ten years) Tj T*
(to the average duration of life was the result of reduced mortality) Tj T*
(in children under five years of age.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The first direct steps towards the reduction of infant mortality) Tj T*
(were directed against epidemic or summer diarrh?a. Medical officers) Tj T*
(of health have always been required in their annual reports to) Tj T*
(summarize the vital statistics in their districts; and since 1905) Tj T*
(a more detailed statement of infant mortality during each part of) Tj T*
(infancy has been required. Annually, therefore, as well as when they) Tj T*
(received the weekly returns of deaths from the local registrars,) Tj T*
(there was forced upon their attention the fact that deaths of infants) Tj T*
(under one year of age formed a high proportion of total deaths at all) Tj T*
(ages \(12.9 per cent. in 1917\), and that of these infantile deaths a) Tj T*
(large proportion were caused by diarrh?a, the number varying with) Tj T*
(the temperature and the deficiency of rainfall in the summer months.) Tj T*
(In 1912, a year of relatively small mortality from diarrh?a, this) Tj T*
(disease caused 8.1 per cent. of all deaths under one year of age.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(For many years past it has been customary for medical officers of) Tj T*
(health to issue warnings as to summer diarrh?a, to arrange for the) Tj T*
(distribution of leaflets of advice concerning the disease, and to) Tj T*
(urge the necessity of more thorough cleanliness both municipal and) Tj T*
(domestic during the summer months. Even before the early notification) Tj T*
(of births became obligatory, in many areas the addresses of infants) Tj T*
(were obtained from the registrars of births and special visits were) Tj T*
(made to the mothers of infants during the months of June and July) Tj T*
(and especially to the mothers of those infants who were known to be) Tj T*
(artificially fed.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The reports of medical officers of health of many of the large) Tj T*
(towns from 1890 onwards show that much valuable work was being) Tj T*
(accomplished, and the way was being prepared for more general) Tj T*
