(The importance of municipal sanitation in aiding the elimination) Tj T*
(of diarrh?al mortality is illustrated in the experience of many) Tj T*
(towns, and strikingly by the comparative experience of Leicester) Tj T*
(and Nottingham. The chief difference between the sanitary condition) Tj T*
(of the two towns was that in Nottingham in 1909 pail closets still) Tj T*
(served more than half the houses, while Leicester had abandoned this) Tj T*
(system entirely, substituting water-closets. Between 1889-93 and 1909) Tj T*
(the diarrh?al mortality in Leicester had declined 52 per cent.; in) Tj T*
(Nottingham it had only declined 4 per cent.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Diarrh?a is not the only disease of infancy which can be greatly) Tj T*
(diminished by improved public health administration. Tuberculosis) Tj T*
(and whooping cough and measles figure largely in the infantile death) Tj T*
(returns. Over 21 per cent. of the total deaths in infancy are due) Tj T*
(to these three diseases and to diarrh?a. The amount of syphilis) Tj T*
(appearing in the death-returns is small; but its actual amount is) Tj T*
(much greater than the figures show. If pneumonia and bronchitis,) Tj T*
(which account for 19 per cent. of the deaths in infancy, be) Tj T*
(regarded?as they should?as infective diseases, then it may be said) Tj T*
(that the problem of saving child life and securing the correlative) Tj T*
(improvement in the standard of health of children who survive to) Tj T*
(higher ages, _consists very largely in the prevention of infections_,) Tj T*
(including diarrh?al diseases and acute respiratory diseases.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It follows from this that even if the limited and erroneous view) Tj T*
(be taken that Sanitary Authorities are concerned only with the) Tj T*
(prevention of infectious diseases, the reduction of infant mortality) Tj T*
(is a duty devolving on these authorities, and cannot be effectively) Tj T*
(carried out without their coöperation. Voluntary effort must) Tj T*
(therefore always, in large measure, be directed towards stimulating) Tj T*
(local authorities to perform their duties.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The influence of diarrh?al summer mortality on the progress of child) Tj T*
(welfare work is further shown by the fact that among the earliest) Tj T*
(efforts were those to provide pure cows? milk to infants. In England) Tj T*
(official Milk Depots for this purpose were never numerous; and) Tj T*
(little voluntary effort went in this direction. There now remain) Tj T*
(very few such Milk Depots; but many local authorities provide milk,) Tj T*
(more particularly dried milk, to infants for whom it is specially) Tj T*
(prescribed at Infant Consultations. Early investigations at Brighton) Tj T*
(and elsewhere showed that the mortality of infants fed on condensed) Tj T*
(milk,?chiefly of the sweetened variety,?was greater than that) Tj T*
(of infants fed on fresh cows? milk, and directed attention to) Tj T*
(the supreme importance of domestic cleanliness in the prevention) Tj T*
(of summer diarrh?a. The Milk Depots and the concurrent agitation) Tj T*
(for purer cows? milk served a useful purpose; though it cannot) Tj T*
(yet be said that the cows? milk ordinarily supplied in England is) Tj T*
(satisfactorily clean.) Tj T*
