(deaths from puerperal sepsis, the death-rate from other complications) Tj T*
(of childbearing has not declined. The decline in puerperal sepsis) Tj T*
(is general throughout the country, and evidences the greater care) Tj T*
(in midwifery both on the part of doctors and of midwives. The) Tj T*
(administration of the Midwives Act, 1902, has doubtless done much) Tj T*
(to secure this. The death-rate from conditions other than puerperal) Tj T*
(fever continues to differ greatly throughout the country. It is) Tj T*
(highest in Welsh counties, Westmoreland, Lancashire and Cheshire) Tj T*
(coming next in order of unfavourable portion; in many industrial,) Tj T*
(including textile, towns it is also excessive. The general conclusion) Tj T*
(reached by the writer in an elaborate official report on the subject) Tj T*
(is that ?the quality and availability of skilled assistance before,) Tj T*
(during, and after childbirth are probably the most important factors) Tj T*
(in determining the remarkable and serious differences in respect of) Tj T*
(mortality from childbearing shown in the report.???The differences) Tj T*
(are caused in the main by differences in availability of skilled) Tj T*
(assistance when needed in pregnancy, and at and after childbirth.?) Tj T*
