() Tj T*
(The Midwives Act, 1918, gave further powers to the Central Midwives) Tj T*
(Board and to local supervising authorities, and made it the duty of) Tj T*
(the latter to pay the fee of a doctor called in by a midwife in any) Tj T*
(of the emergencies for which Rules are made by the Central Midwives) Tj T*
(Board, the fee paid to be in accordance with a scale prescribed by) Tj T*
(the Ministry of Health.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(As at least three-fourths of the total births in England and Wales) Tj T*
(are attended by midwives with or without the assistance of doctors,) Tj T*
(their work has great importance in relation to the reduction of) Tj T*
(maternal disablement and mortality and to the prevention of early) Tj T*
(infant mortality, and it is of happy augury that they are being) Tj T*
(enlisted more and more in official work for safeguarding the health) Tj T*
(of the mother and her unborn or recently delivered infant. An) Tj T*
(important recent addition has been made to the rules of the Central) Tj T*
(Midwives Board, which makes it obligatory on the midwife to notify) Tj T*
(to the medical officer of health any instance, while the patient is) Tj T*
(under her charge, in which for any reason breast-feeding has been) Tj T*
(discontinued.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Administrative Work._?Largely through the machinery provided by) Tj T*
(the Midwives Act and the Notification of Births Act a system of) Tj T*
(supervision of maternity and child welfare has been organized in) Tj T*
(every county and county borough, and this has been responsible for) Tj T*
(a large share of the improvement experienced in recent years. The) Tj T*
(character and extent of development of the work varies greatly in) Tj T*
(different centres; and as a rule the work is more fully developed) Tj T*
(in county boroughs than in counties. In county districts it has) Tj T*
(sometimes been found necessary to unite the offices of assistant) Tj T*
(inspector of midwives, infant visitor and tuberculosis visitor in one) Tj T*
(adequately trained health visitor, thus saving time in travelling) Tj T*
(by enabling the visitor to have a smaller district allotted to her) Tj T*
(than if she undertook only one branch of work. In some counties the) Tj T*
(school nurse?s work is also undertaken by the health visitor. In some) Tj T*
(country areas arrangements have been made for infant visiting to be) Tj T*
(carried out by district nurses who are also midwives.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Voluntary Workers._?Much of the success so far achieved in improving) Tj T*
(the health conditions of infancy and childhood has been secured by) Tj T*
(coöperation between voluntary and official health visitors. Excellent) Tj T*
(work has been done by local and other societies, particularly during) Tj T*
(the last ten years, in educating public opinion and in direct) Tj T*
(assistance to mothers and their infants. It is essential that such) Tj T*
(voluntary work should have a nucleus of highly trained and well-paid) Tj T*
(workers; but given this condition, a large amount of good work can be) Tj T*
(accomplished by voluntary aid.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
