(                _Training and Provision of Midwives_) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The provision of additional trained midwives is a pressing problem.) Tj T*
(The increased cost of living, longer training required, and the) Tj T*
(rapid development of less laborious and more lucrative occupations,) Tj T*
(have made it difficult to secure women to train as midwives, or to) Tj T*
(continue to practise in this capacity after qualification. In many) Tj T*
(industrial areas the older _bonâ fide_ midwife is preferred, although) Tj T*
(it is the almost universal experience that the trained midwife more) Tj T*
(quickly detects conditions endangering the life of the mother or) Tj T*
(infant, and sends for medical help. In order to encourage further) Tj T*
(the supply of practising midwives, the government gives grants) Tj T*
(for increased remuneration to midwives newly appointed by local) Tj T*
(authorities, sufficient to recoup them in the course of a few years?) Tj T*
(service for the cost of their training.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(At a recent date, of some 30,543 trained midwives on the Roll, only) Tj T*
(6,754 were returned as being in actual practice as such.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In order to make midwives available for all women needing them, the) Tj T*
(Board repays to local authorities and voluntary associations half the) Tj T*
(cost of the provision of a midwife for necessitous women. During the) Tj T*
(Great War a woman might receive assistance in her confinement from) Tj T*
(several central sources; for in addition to the above) Tj T*
