(On July 30, 1914, the Local Government Board sent a circular letter) Tj T*
(and a covering memorandum by their Medical Officer which may be) Tj T*
(claimed to have been the starting point of maternity and child) Tj T*
(welfare work on a larger scale, more generally distributed throughout) Tj T*
(the country, and more completely covering the whole sphere of) Tj T*
(medical and hygienic work for this purpose than had previously been) Tj T*
(envisaged. Although the country at that time might be said to be) Tj T*
(already under the shadow of war, these documents had been previously) Tj T*
(prepared, and their appearance four days before the declaration) Tj T*
(of war was a coincidence. The chief burden of the additional work) Tj T*
(to which local authorities were urged was that there should be) Tj T*
(_continuity in dealing with the whole period from before birth until) Tj T*
(the time when the child is entered upon a school register_; and the) Tj T*
(memorandum contemplated that ?medical advice and, where necessary,) Tj T*
(treatment should be continuously and systematically available for) Tj T*
(expectant mothers and for children till they are entered on a school) Tj T*
(register, and that arrangements should be made for home visitation) Tj T*
(throughout this period.? It was added that ?the work of home) Tj T*
(visitation is one to which the Board attach very great importance and) Tj T*
(in promoting schemes laid down in the accompanying memorandum the) Tj T*
(first step should be the appointment of an adequate staff of health) Tj T*
(visitors.?) Tj T*
